<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">originated during slavery; first published April <a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen&quot; is an African-American spiritual that took shape during the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> but was not published until after emancipation. It first appeared in print, as &quot;Nobody Knows&quot;, with seven verses, in April 1866 in The Child at Home, a paper of the American Tract Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>.</p></div>
